</span></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">7.2.2 </span>Varying Quartiles.</h4>

<div id="S7.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To determine the optimal split index <math id="S7.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{split}</annotation></semantics></math>, Terraform searches in its Inter-Quartile Range (IQR) for an index that minimizes the intra-split variance (<math id="S7.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Var_{intra}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Var</mi><mi>intra</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Var_{intra}}</annotation></semantics></math>).
A brute-force approach to determine the split index <math id="S7.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{split}</annotation></semantics></math> is to iterate over each index in the set of client dataset sizes, <math id="S7.SS2.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\mathcal{H\text{(sorted)}}}_{\text{train}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mtext>train</mtext><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>(sorted)</mtext></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\mathcal{H\text{(sorted)}}}_{\text{train}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and calculate the intra-split variance.
Such an approach would be computationally expensive, and IQR minimizes this cost by reducing the search space:
from the total set (0,1) to the region between the first and third quartiles of the set (Q1, Q3).</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In Figure <a href="#S7.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 7.2.1 Varying Client Updates. ‣ 7.2 Ablation Studies ‣ 7 Evaluation ‣ Heterogeneity-Aware Client Selection Methodology For Efficient Federated Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, on the CIFAR100 and Tiny ImageNet datasets for <math id="S7.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math> rounds, we compare IQR (Q1, Q3) against three other splitting ranges:
(0, 1), (0, Q3), and (Q1, 1).
Our results illustrate that IQR is not only sufficient for determining split index <math id="S7.SS2.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{split}</annotation></semantics></math>, but also helps to discard outliers that reduce the test accuracy.
Additionally, (Q3, 1) have the poorest performance, which validates the theory that hyper-focusing on only highly heterogeneous clients overfits the model and reduces generalization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training time:</span> In Figure <a href="#S7.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 7.2.1 Varying Client Updates. ‣ 7.2 Ablation Studies ‣ 7 Evaluation ‣ Heterogeneity-Aware Client Selection Methodology For Efficient Federated Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we measure the training time complexity for the 4 quartile ranges and observe that our claims that IQR and total set have the lowest and highest training time, respectively, hold.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">7.2.3 </span>Threshold for <math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\#" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>#</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\#</annotation></semantics></math>Clients (<math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>).</h4>

<div id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Terraform’s hierarchical sampling approach iteratively splits the set of clients into easy <math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> and hard <math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> clusters.
Specifically, we keep splitting the hard cluster until the following termination condition is met: the size of the hard cluster falls below a predefined threshold (<math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>).
Determining an optimal value of <math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> saves compute as further splitting will yield minimal benefits.
Thus, we set <math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>.
In Table <a href="#S7.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 7.2.1 Varying Client Updates. ‣ 7.2 Ablation Studies ‣ 7 Evaluation ‣ Heterogeneity-Aware Client Selection Methodology For Efficient Federated Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we illustrate the accuracy on three different values for <math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=\{2,3,4\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=\{2,3,4\}</annotation></semantics></math> on FEMNIST and FMNIST (1 and 4*) scenarios.
We observe that <math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=4</annotation></semantics></math> performs well on both datasets.
Although <math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=2</annotation></semantics></math> yields a marginally better accuracy on the FEMNIST dataset, <math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=4</annotation></semantics></math> reduces the computational cost for training and yields only <math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.11\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.11</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.11\%</annotation></semantics></math> lower accuracy than <math id="S7.SS2.SSS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=2</annotation></semantics></math>, which makes it an optimal choice.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Conclusions</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we presented Terraform, a novel client selection methodology that mitigates the impact of heterogeneous clients on federated learning.
Terraform achieves this goal by hierarchically selecting and retraining heterogeneous clients.
Terraform performs this hierarchical selection on the basis of clients’ local model’s final-layer gradient updates, which it uses to determine the optimal
inter-quartile range (IQR) to split clients into easy and hard clusters.
Terraform retrains clients in hard clusters and our results illustrate that it outperforms existing works by up to <math id="S8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="47\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>47</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">47\%</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
